    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 205-20, a component qualifies for discontinued operations presentation when its disposal represents a strategic shift that has or will have a major effect on the entity's operations and financial results. The medical device division meets this threshold — the board determined it represents a strategic shift, and the held-for-sale criteria are met. The financing delay does not disqualify it; ASC 205-20 does not impose a one-year expiration on held-for-sale classification, and entities may remain in held-for-sale status beyond one year if the delay is caused by events beyond the entity's control. Meanwhile, the consumer product line sale generating only 3% of total revenue does not represent a strategic shift — it is a routine disposal of a small portion of a continuing segment. Under ASC 205-20, only disposals representing a strategic shift qualify for discontinued operations; minor product line dispositions are reported within continuing operations. Business interpretation: the controller must distinguish between a component that represents a significant change in the entity's operations (discontinued operations) and a routine divestiture of a minor asset group (continuing operations). The classification affects how users of financial statements assess the entity's ongoing earning power — mixing minor divestitures into discontinued operations would overstate the earnings from continuing operations and mislead users about the sustainability of current period results. A common exam trap is to apply a mechanical one-year rule to held-for-sale classification — no such automatic expiration exists under ASC 205-20. Another trap is treating all disposals as discontinued operations regardless of materiality.",
