    "Stem": "CONTROLLER'S MEMORANDUM — Apex Industrial Controls\n\nTO: CFO Priya Mehta\nFROM: Elena Vasquez, Controller\nRE: Revenue Recognition for ApexGuard 9000 Customer Contracts\n\nApex sells the ApexGuard 9000, a programmable safety controller for chemical processing plants, under a standard customer contract with four components: (1) the hardware controller unit, delivered at contract signing for $180,000; (2) a three-year proprietary software license with quarterly security updates, bundled at $72,000; (3) on-site installation and calibration services performed over a two-week period following delivery, invoiced at $48,000; and (4) a two-year maintenance and technical support plan for $36,000. The controller and software license are sold separately to customers who already own ApexGuard hardware. Installation is performed by Apex-certified technicians and is also available as a standalone service. The software updates require Apex's proprietary encryption keys and cannot be performed by the customer or third parties. The maintenance plan includes 24/7 phone support and annual on-site inspections. Total contract price: $336,000. Ms. Mehta asks: 'How many performance obligations does this contract contain under ASC 606, and what is the basis for our determination?'",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Four distinct performance obligations — hardware, software license, installation, and maintenance — because each is separately identifiable and the customer can benefit from each on its own or with readily available resources, and each is distinct within the context of the contract because the promises are not inputs to a combined output.",
      "B": "One combined performance obligation because all four components are part of a single integrated safety system solution. The customer purchased a complete chemical plant safety capability, and Apex should recognize revenue as a single performance obligation at the point when the system is fully operational.",
      "C": "Three performance obligations — hardware, bundled software-plus-installation, and maintenance — because installation and the software license are highly interdependent. The software cannot function without the hardware being properly installed and calibrated, so installation and the software license should be combined into a single performance obligation.",
      "D": "Two performance obligations — the hardware-plus-installation as one obligation (since the hardware must be installed to function) and the software-plus-maintenance as a second obligation (since the software updates and support are ongoing services). The bundled pricing makes individual separation impractical."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 606-10-25-14, a performance obligation is a promise to transfer a distinct good or service to the customer. A good or service is distinct if two criteria are met: (1) the customer can benefit from the good or service on its own or together with readily available resources (capable-of-being-distinct criterion), and (2) the entity's promise to transfer the good or service is separately identifiable from other promises in the contract (distinct-within-the-context-of-the-contract criterion). All four components meet both criteria. The hardware controller is a standalone product sold separately to existing ApexGuard customers — a customer who already owns the hardware could purchase just the hardware from Apex or a competitor, satisfying criterion 1. The proprietary software license with quarterly updates is also sold independently to customers who already have ApexGuard hardware, and customers can benefit from the license with the hardware they already own. The installation services are performed by Apex-certified technicians and are available as a standalone service — a customer could purchase hardware from Apex and hire an Apex-certified technician for installation under a separate arrangement. The maintenance plan is clearly distinct as an ongoing service that continues well after delivery and installation. Within the context of the contract, these four promises are not inputs to a combined output — the customer is purchasing discrete deliverables, not a single integrated item. The hardware can function without the software (a competitor's software could run on ApexGuard hardware), the software can run without Apex's maintenance plan, and installation is a one-time service that does not transform the other components. Therefore, the contract contains four distinct performance obligations, each requiring separate revenue recognition timing under the five-step model. A critical exam trap is concluding that multiple deliverables in a single contract automatically constitute one combined performance obligation — ASC 606 explicitly requires disaggregation when promises are distinct. Candidates should remember that 'separately sold' is strong evidence of distinctness, and bundled pricing does not, by itself, combine performance obligations.",
