    "Stem": "CONTROLLER MEMORANDUM — Harbor Medical Supplies\n\nTO: Controller Jia Patel\nFROM: Internal Audit\nRE: Year-End Allowance for Doubtful Accounts — Forward-Looking Analysis\n\nHarbor carries $4,200,000 in gross trade receivables. The historical loss rate (5-year average) is 2.1% on current, 6.4% on 31-60 days, 15.2% on 61-90 days, and 38.7% on >90 days. However, this year's aging shows significant shifts: (1) the >90-day bucket doubled to $420,000 — three major hospital clients deferred payments citing 'revenue cycle disruptions'; (2) the 61-90 day bucket grew from 12% to 19% of the portfolio; (3) one client ($180,000 in the 31-60 bucket) filed Chapter 11 in January before issuance date. The Fed raised rates 125 bps this year, and the Hospital CFO Council reports industry-wide DSOs at a 7-year high. Patel's deputy recommends applying historical loss rates without adjustment, arguing that objectivity requires mechanical application of aging-based rates. The external auditors note ASC 326 (CECL) requires consideration of 'current conditions and reasonable and supportable forecasts.' Patel asks: 'Which judgment about the allowance is most defensible under ASC 326?'",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Apply the historical loss rates mechanically without adjustment to each aging bucket. The aging analysis is the primary measurement tool, and subjective forward-looking adjustments undermine consistency and comparability. Total allowance: $324,600.",
      "B": "Adjust historical loss rates upward by incorporating forward-looking information (Fed rate increases, industry DSO trends, Chapter 11 filing) and apply higher rates to the shifted aging categories. Under ASC 326, the controller must exercise judgment that a purely mechanical application no longer reflects the current expected credit loss given current conditions.",
      "C": "Recognize the Chapter 11 client as fully impaired with a specific $180,000 write-off, but apply historical loss rates to other receivables. Rate environment and industry trends are too uncertain and subjective to quantify, and ASC 326 does not require speculative adjustments. Total allowance: approximately $372,000.",
      "D": "Maintain historical loss rates for the <90-day buckets but double the >90-day rate from 38.7% to 77.4% because the bucket doubled in size. The controller should adjust rates only when aging composition changes measurably, as portfolio shifts are the most objective signal of credit deterioration. Total allowance: approximately $387,000."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 326-20 (Current Expected Credit Losses), the allowance for credit losses must reflect the current estimate of expected credit losses over the contractual life of the financial asset. ASC 326-20-30-6 requires entities to consider relevant available information about past events, current conditions, and reasonable and supportable forecasts. The FASB explicitly rejected a purely mechanical or formula-based approach. Key analysis: (1) Historical loss rates are the starting point, not the conclusion — ASC 326 mandates that historical experience be adjusted for current conditions and forward-looking information. (2) The 125 bps Fed rate increase makes debt service more expensive for Harbor's hospital clients, increasing default risk. (3) Industry-wide DSOs at 7-year highs represent objective evidence of broad-based collection deterioration across the hospital supply sector. (4) The Chapter 11 filing, while occurring after year-end, provides additional evidence about conditions that existed at the balance sheet date (the client's financial distress was a pre-existing condition). (5) The aging composition shift (doubled >90-day and expanded 61-90-day buckets) is itself an objective signal that the historical loss rate by age category may be stale. Under ASC 326, the controller has an affirmative obligation to incorporate all available information — current conditions and reasonable forecasts — not merely to default to historical averages. An exam trap is applying the pre-CECL incurred loss model (ASC 450-20) which relied primarily on historical experience; CECL replaced that approach with a forward-looking expected loss model. The controller should estimate total allowance in the range of approximately $410,000-$445,000, reflecting adjusted rates that account for current deterioration signals while maintaining a reasonable and supportable basis for each adjustment.",
