    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "CONTROLLER MEMORANDUM — Titan Industrial Supply\n\nTO: CFO Alan Ross\nFROM: Controller's Office\nRE: Q3 LIFO Liquidation — Income Effect and Disclosure Assessment\n\nTitan uses dollar-value LIFO for its steel products division. A 9-week supplier strike in Q3 forced Titan to draw down inventory from 48,000 units (December 31 base) to 14,200 units by September 30, piercing LIFO layers dating back to 2018. The relevant LIFO layers are: 2018 layer: 11,500 units at $42.00/unit; 2019 layer: 9,800 units at $45.80/unit; 2020 layer: 12,300 units at $51.30/unit; 2021 layer: 8,200 units at $58.75/unit; 2022 layer: 6,200 units at $67.20/unit. Current replacement cost is $71.50/unit, and Titan's Q3 sales price averaged $89.00/unit. Before the liquidation, Q3 projected operating margin was 14.2%. The external auditors have flagged this for disclosure evaluation, noting that the LIFO liquidation gain may be material. Titan expects to replenish inventory in Q4 once the strike ends. Mr. Ross asks: 'What is the Q3 P&L impact of this LIFO liquidation, and what disclosure is required?'",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The liquidation releases older, lower-cost LIFO layers into COGS (replacing $71.50 current cost with costs as low as $42.00), increasing Q3 gross profit. Disclosure is required under ASC 270-10-50-6 because the non-recurring gain materially distorts comparability of interim operating results. However, the gain is embedded in COGS — it is NOT presented as a separate income statement line item but disclosed in the notes to the interim financial statements.",
      "B": "The liquidation has no P&L impact because LIFO is a cost flow assumption, not a physical flow. Titan will replenish inventory in Q4, restoring the LIFO layers, so the Q3 liquidation is a temporary phenomenon that self-corrects. No separate disclosure is required; Titan should note the strike in MD&A without quantifying the LIFO effect.",
      "C": "The liquidation reduces Q3 COGS and increases operating margin, and the income effect must be presented as a separate line item on the income statement ('LIFO liquidation gain') below gross profit. ASC 270 requires separate presentation of material and unusual items. The gain is recognized in Q3 and reverses when inventory is replenished in Q4, with the Q4 reversal also separately presented.",
      "D": "Titan should neutralize the income effect by recording a LIFO liquidation reserve equal to the difference between replacement cost and the liquidated layer costs (debit COGS, credit LIFO liquidation reserve). Under the matching principle, COGS should reflect current replacement cost regardless of LIFO layers; the reserve defers the liquidation gain until inventory is physically replaced."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under LIFO, when inventory quantities drop below prior-year levels, older, lower-cost LIFO layers are released into cost of goods sold, replacing the current-period higher replacement costs that would otherwise have been charged to COGS. This is a LIFO liquidation. The income effect is the difference between the replacement cost of the units sold from older layers and the historical cost at which those layers were carried. The P&L impact flows through a reduced COGS, which increases gross profit and operating margin — but the gain is embedded in COGS, not presented as a separate line item. Regarding disclosure: ASC 270-10-50-6 requires interim financial statements to disclose material events or transactions that affect the comparability of interim results. A LIFO liquidation that materializes from a non-recurring event (strike) and significantly distorts reported operating margin meets this threshold because users comparing Q3 Year 2 to Q3 Year 1 would see an inflated margin that does not reflect sustainable operating performance. The SEC also requires disclosure of material LIFO liquidations (Regulation S-X, Rule 5-02-6(b)). The appropriate disclosure is a note quantifying the LIFO liquidation effect on cost of goods sold and net income, allowing financial statement users to distinguish operating results from the non-recurring inventory liquidation gain. A critical exam trap is confusing separate income statement presentation (not required — the gain is embedded in COGS) with note disclosure (required if material). Another trap is treating the Q4 replenishment as automatically reversing the Q3 income effect — replacement at current prices creates a new LIFO layer at current cost; the Q3 lower COGS is recognized in Q3 regardless of subsequent replenishment.",
