    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under IAS 38 (Intangible Assets), development costs must be capitalized as an intangible asset when an entity can demonstrate all of the following: technical feasibility of completing the asset, intention to complete and use or sell it, ability to use or sell the asset, how the asset will generate future economic benefits, availability of resources to complete development, and ability to measure the expenditure reliably. When these criteria are met (as stated for Axion's $3.2 million in qualifying costs), capitalization better matches the costs with the future revenues the software will generate — a core principle of accrual accounting. Under U.S. GAAP (ASC 730 and ASC 985-20), most development costs are expensed, although software costs may be capitalized once technological feasibility is established. For Axion, IFRS provides a more faithful representation of economic performance because the $3.2 million represents genuine future economic benefits from successful development efforts. The controller should recommend IFRS reporting to better communicate Axion's investment in innovation and the asset value created by its development activities.",
