    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 230-10-20, cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments that are: (a) readily convertible to known amounts of cash, and (b) so near their maturity that they present insignificant risk of changes in value because of changes in interest rates. Generally, only investments with original maturities of three months or less qualify. The analysis for each instrument: Item 1 (13-week T-bill, 2 weeks remaining): Original maturity at issuance = 91 days (≤ 3 months). Qualifies as cash equivalent. Item 2 (120-day commercial paper, 30 days remaining): Original maturity is 120 days — exceeds the 3-month threshold. Remaining maturity to Keystone at acquisition date does not override the original maturity test. The key criterion under ASC 230 is the original maturity to the entity holding the investment. Under U.S. GAAP, investments with original maturities > 3 months are generally classified as short-term investments, not cash equivalents, even if purchased close to maturity. This is the most common exam trap. Item 3 (Money market fund with redemption gate): While money market funds are commonly treated as cash equivalents, the existence of a redemption gate provision creates a restriction on the availability of funds. If the gate can be imposed and would prevent same-day redemption, the instrument is not 'readily convertible to known amounts of cash.' The fact that the fund is currently at 34% (above 30%) does not eliminate the gate risk — the classification assessment considers the instrument's characteristics, not just current conditions. A restricted money market fund is classified as a short-term investment. Item 4 (6-month CD, 75 days remaining): Original maturity is 6 months (183 days) — exceeds 3-month threshold. Early withdrawal penalty further indicates the instrument is not 'near maturity.' Classify as short-term investment. Item 5 (90-day T-bill, 87 days remaining): Original maturity at issuance = 90 days (≤ 3 months). Purchased in secondary market does not change the original maturity. Qualifies as cash equivalent. A critical exam trap is confusing remaining maturity with original maturity. Original maturity is measured from issuance to the holder, not from acquisition date to maturity date. Another trap: assuming all money market funds are cash equivalents — funds with redemption restrictions (gates, fees, suspension provisions) must be evaluated for liquidity restrictions.",
