    "ExplanationWrongA": "This recommendation misclassifies item 2 (120-day commercial paper) as a cash equivalent based on remaining maturity rather than original maturity. Under ASC 230-10-20, the classification test uses the investment's original maturity to the holder, not the remaining maturity at the balance sheet date. Commercial paper with a 120-day original maturity at issuance exceeds the 3-month threshold, regardless of how close to maturity Keystone purchased it or how much time remains. The recommendation's treatment of item 3 (money market fund) is also questionable — a fund with a redemption gate provision is not the same as an unrestricted fund. While the gate is not currently activated (34% is above the 30% trigger), U.S. GAAP and SEC guidance focus on the instrument's inherent characteristics, not the probability of gate activation. A treasury analyst who classifies all money market funds identically regardless of redemption restrictions risks overstating cash and cash equivalents.",
