    "Stem": "INTERNAL MEMO — Orion Precision Components\n\nTO: Controller Elena Vasquez\nFROM: Audit Partner James Okonkwo\nRE: Depreciation Analysis Required Before Sign-Off\n\nOrion acquired CNC machining equipment on March 1 for $124,800, with estimated salvage of $12,000 and a 7-year useful life. The audit partner has flagged that the prior-year depreciation was calculated inconsistently and wants a documented analysis before signing the audit opinion. Three issues have emerged:\n\n(1) The production engineer argues that since the equipment runs 24/7 at 85% capacity utilization and will maintain that rate for its entire life, straight-line depreciation most accurately reflects the consumption pattern.\n(2) The CFO's preliminary earnings release to analysts projected depreciation of approximately $18,000/year — a figure that the controller's office cannot reproduce using any standard GAAP method with the given inputs. The earnings release went out last week and a correction could affect the stock price.\n(3) The tax department applies MACRS but acknowledges this is separate from GAAP reporting.\n\nWith the audit opinion deadline in 48 hours, Ms. Vasquez must determine: what is the correct GAAP straight-line depreciation expense, and can the CFO's $18,000 projection be justified under any permissible method?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Annual straight-line depreciation is $16,114 [($124,800 - $12,000) / 7]. The CFO's $18,000 projection cannot be justified under any standard GAAP method with these inputs — it is approximately $1,886 above the correct straight-line amount. The closest defensible alternative would allocate cost without salvage ($124,800 / 7 = $17,829), but this violates ASC 360-10-35-4 which requires salvage value to be excluded from the depreciable base. The controller should advise the CFO that the earnings release figure needs correction.",
      "B": "Annual straight-line depreciation is $17,829 ($124,800 / 7). The controller should support the CFO's $18,000 projection because salvage estimates for CNC equipment are inherently unreliable given rapid technological obsolescence, and the difference between $17,829 and $18,000 ($171) is immaterial for a company of Orion's size. The controller should recommend maintaining the earnings release figure to avoid a stock price disruption over an immaterial rounding difference.",
      "C": "Annual straight-line depreciation is $112,800 / 6 = $18,800, approximately matching the CFO's projection. The 7-year useful life is for book purposes only; economic depreciation should use a 6-year life to reflect actual technological obsolescence in the CNC machining industry. The controller can justify the $18,000 figure by arguing for a shorter useful life under ASC 360-10-35-4.",
      "D": "Annual straight-line depreciation is ($124,800 - $12,000) / 6.5 = $17,354, reflecting a mid-year averaging adjustment. The CFO's projection of $18,000 is close and within acceptable rounding tolerance. The controller should recommend holding the earnings release figure and adjusting it prospectively in Year 2 when better utilization data is available."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 360-10-35-4, straight-line depreciation allocates the depreciable base (cost less salvage) evenly over the asset's useful life. Orion's depreciable cost = $124,800 - $12,000 = $112,800. Annual depreciation = $112,800 / 7 = $16,114 (rounded to nearest dollar). The CFO's $18,000 projection cannot be reconciled to any GAAP-compliant calculation with these inputs: (a) $124,800 / 7 = $17,829 (ignores salvage, violates ASC 360-10-35-4), (b) ($124,800 - $12,000) / 6 = $18,800 (uses wrong useful life, no basis in the equipment specifications), (c) mid-year averaging is not a GAAP depreciation method. The controller has an ethical obligation to correct the earnings release before the audit opinion is issued. The 24/7 utilization at 85% capacity does not affect the consumption pattern — the asset produces the same productive capacity each year regardless of utilization rate, and straight-line is therefore the correct reflection of benefit consumption. A common exam trap is dividing cost by useful life without deducting salvage; another is adjusting useful life without a change in estimate supported by new information per ASC 250-10-45-17. Business consequence: failure to correct could result in a material misstatement, potential SEC investigation if the discrepancy appears in filed reports, and erosion of audit committee confidence. The controller should advise the CFO of the $16,114 correct figure and recommend issuing a corrected earnings release.",
