    "Stem": "CONTROLLER'S ASSESSMENT MEMO — Crestline Manufacturing\n\nTO: CFO Maria Okonkwo\nFROM: David Tran, Controller\nRE: FY2025-FY2027 Budget Performance Review and FY2028 Budget Process Recommendation\n\nCrestline operates four divisions: Commercial Components, Consumer Products, Industrial Systems, and Aftermarket Services. Since FY2022, Crestline has used pure top-down budgeting: corporate finance sets division-level revenue and cost targets based on prior-year actuals adjusted by corporate GDP growth assumptions. I have completed a three-year audit comparing divisional budget targets against actual results:\n\n| Division | FY2025 Variance | FY2026 Variance | FY2027 Variance | 3-Year Cumulative |\n|----------|----------------|----------------|----------------|--------------------|\n| Commercial Components | ($380,000) U | ($520,000) U | ($610,000) U | ($1,510,000) U |\n| Consumer Products | ($180,000) F | ($220,000) U | ($340,000) U | ($380,000) U |\n| Industrial Systems | ($90,000) U | ($160,000) F | ($240,000) U | ($170,000) U |\n| Aftermarket Services | ($60,000) U | ($80,000) U | ($140,000) F | $0 |\n| **Company Total** | **($710,000) U** | **($660,000) U** | **($1,050,000) U** | **($2,060,000) U** |\n\nThree patterns are evident: (1) The two largest divisions (Commercial Components and Consumer Products) show worsening unfavorable trends — their combined FY2027 miss of $950,000 is 44% larger than FY2025. (2) Division controllers reported in anonymous surveys (response rate 78%) that corporate cost inflation assumptions — a uniform 2.5% applied to all divisions — understated actual supplier cost increases of 5-9% in specialized materials. (3) Voluntary turnover among division finance managers reached 28% in FY2027, with exit interviews citing frustration at being evaluated against targets they had no input in setting. The Board has demanded an explanation for the $2.06 million cumulative shortfall and expects a budget process recommendation before the FY2028 budget cycle begins in eight weeks. Which recommendation should the Controller present to the CFO?",
