    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Participative budgeting with corporate review and final approval authority (Choice B) is the recommendation best supported by Crestline's three-year evidence. The core problem is information asymmetry: corporate finance used uniform 2.5% inflation assumptions when actual divisional supplier costs rose 5-9%, producing the $1.51 million cumulative miss in Commercial Components alone. Division controllers knew their supplier costs better but had no mechanism to provide that knowledge under pure top-down budgeting. Participative budgeting corrects this by capturing local operational intelligence during budget preparation — managers who negotiate supplier contracts, understand capacity constraints, and track customer demand produce more accurate input. However, participation introduces budgetary slack risk — managers may understate achievable revenue or overstate required resources if their compensation is tied to meeting budget targets. The solution is NOT unfiltered bottom-up budgeting (Choice C, which abandons the control function) or complete decentralization (Choice D, which eliminates strategic coordination). It is participative budgeting with corporate review authority: division managers propose, corporate finance evaluates for reasonableness and strategic alignment, adjustments are communicated with rationale, and approved budgets become the performance benchmark. This preserves the control and authorization functions of the budget — spending limits, resource allocation, and accountability — while incorporating the information quality that pure top-down systematically misses. The 28% division finance manager turnover adds a human capital dimension: participation improves commitment and retention by giving managers genuine input into the targets they are evaluated against. A common exam trap is treating the budget-approach decision as a binary between pure top-down (Choice A, which merely refines flawed corporate assumptions) and unfiltered bottom-up (Choice D). The CMA exam expects candidates to recognize that effective budgeting blends operational knowledge with strategic oversight.",
