    "Stem": "CONTROLLER'S MEMO — Northfield Consumer Products\n\nTO: Sarah Kessler, Controller\nFROM: Budget Office\nRE: ZBB Pilot Department Selection for FY2027\n\nNorthfield manufactures branded household cleaning products. The CEO has mandated that every support function reduce its controllable costs by a minimum of 8% for FY2027. After reviewing the cost-reduction options, the Budget Office has determined that two of Northfield's five support departments should pilot zero-based budgeting (ZBB) for FY2027, with the remaining three continuing under the current incremental budgeting approach. The table below summarizes each department's cost profile:\n\nDept | 3-Year Cost Growth Rate | Discretionary Spend % | Current Annual Budget | FY2027 Incremental Request\nA — Marketing | +4.1% | 62% | $3,840,000 | +$197,000 (5.1%)\nB — IT Services | +21.8% | 74% | $5,220,000 | +$415,000 (8.0%)\nC — Human Resources | +5.6% | 31% | $1,960,000 | +$112,000 (5.7%)\nD — Facilities Mgmt | +18.4% | 45% | $2,880,000 | +$388,000 (13.5%)\nE — Legal | +9.2% | 19% | $1,440,000 | +$84,000 (5.8%)\n\nKey considerations: ZBB requires managers to justify every dollar from a zero base. It is most effective in departments with high discretionary spending and high cost growth rates. It is resource-intensive; piloting in two departments allows lessons learned before enterprise-wide rollout. Which two departments should Sarah Kessler recommend for the ZBB pilot?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Marketing (A) and Facilities Management (D) — Marketing has the highest absolute budget ($3.84M) and Facilities has the highest FY2027 percentage increase request (13.5%), making them the highest-dollar-impact candidates.",
      "B": "IT Services (B) and Facilities Management (D) — these two departments combine the highest 3-year cost growth rates (+21.8% and +18.4%) with the highest and third-highest discretionary spend percentages (74% and 45%), indicating that prior-year spending levels are least likely to reflect genuinely required future spending.",
      "C": "IT Services (B) and Marketing (A) — these two departments have the largest absolute budgets ($5.22M and $3.84M respectively), and capturing ZBB savings on the largest cost bases will generate the greatest dollar savings for the 8% reduction mandate.",
      "D": "Human Resources (C) and Legal (E) — these two departments have the lowest 3-year cost growth rates and the lowest discretionary spending percentages, meaning their budgets are already lean and ZBB review would confirm appropriate spending levels rather than disrupting essential operations."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The two best ZBB pilot candidates are IT Services (B) and Facilities Management (D). The selection logic rests on two ZBB principles. First, ZBB provides the greatest benefit in departments with high discretionary spending — spending not automatically determined by prior-year levels or contractual obligations. IT Services at 74% discretionary and Facilities Management at 45% discretionary have substantial portions of their budgets that are not locked into mandatory spending, meaning a zero-base review can identify genuine opportunities to eliminate, reduce, or restructure activities. Second, departments with the highest cost growth rates (+21.8% for IT, +18.4% for Facilities) are the most likely to have accumulated spending under incremental budgeting that would not survive a zero-base justification. The combination of high growth and high discretionary spending creates the strongest case for ZBB: these departments have both the opportunity (flexible spending) and the indication (rapid growth) that current spending levels exceed necessary spending levels. Marketing (A) has high discretionary spending (62%) but very low cost growth (4.1%), suggesting its current spending trajectory is already controlled. Legal (E) has low discretionary spending (19%) because most legal costs are driven by litigation, compliance requirements, and contractual obligations — ZBB would add process cost without substantial savings opportunity. Human Resources (C) has moderate cost growth (5.6%) and low discretionary spending (31%), making it a poor ZBB candidate. Absolute budget size is not the selection driver — ZBB targets the spending that is most likely unjustified, which correlates with high growth rates in discretionary categories rather than large budgets in categories where spending is largely non-discretionary.",
