    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "POST-IMPLEMENTATION REVIEW — Harborview Medical Center\n\nTO: Audit Committee\nFROM: Internal Audit Department\nRE: ZBB Pilot — One-Year Assessment and Expansion Recommendation\n\nHarborview implemented zero-based budgeting (ZBB) in three support departments for FY2026: Radiology Administration, Facilities Management, and Human Resources. The remaining five support departments continued under incremental budgeting. The pilot's goal was to determine whether ZBB should be expanded enterprise-wide for FY2027. After one year, Internal Audit has compiled the following results:\n\nDepartment | Budget Method | FY2025 Cost (Pre-ZBB) | FY2026 Cost | Cost Change | Manager Hours Spent on Budget Prep | % of Budget Deemed Discretionary\nRadiology Admin | ZBB | $2,840,000 | $2,510,000 | -11.6% | 420 hours | 58%\nFacilities Mgmt | ZBB | $3,620,000 | $2,980,000 | -17.7% | 510 hours | 64%\nHuman Resources | ZBB | $1,940,000 | $1,810,000 | -6.7% | 380 hours | 31%\nIT Services | Incremental | $4,120,000 | $4,390,000 | +6.6% | 180 hours | 72%\nFinance | Incremental | $2,260,000 | $2,420,000 | +7.1% | 160 hours | 28%\nMarketing | Incremental | $3,180,000 | $3,370,000 | +6.0% | 200 hours | 63%\n\nKey observations: Radiology and Facilities achieved double-digit cost reductions. HR showed modest improvement. The three ZBB departments averaged 437 hours of manager time on budget preparation vs. 180 hours for incremental departments. Two department heads (Radiology, Facilities) rated the process as 'worth the effort'; HR rated it 'burdensome relative to savings.' IT Services and Marketing, which remained on incremental budgeting, grew costs 6-7% despite having the highest discretionary spend percentages. The CEO must decide whether to expand ZBB enterprise-wide for FY2027. Based on the pilot evidence, which recommendation should Internal Audit present to the Audit Committee?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Recommend enterprise-wide ZBB adoption for all eight support departments. The combined 12% average cost reduction in pilot departments ($1.1M in savings) demonstrates ZBB's effectiveness, and the incremental-budgeting departments grew costs 6-7% — evidence that continuing incremental budgeting will not achieve cost discipline.",
      "B": "Recommend expanding ZBB to IT Services and Marketing but retaining incremental budgeting for Finance, HR, and the two already-optimized ZBB departments. IT and Marketing combine high discretionary spending (72% and 63%) with cost growth under incremental budgeting — the profile where ZBB's cost-benefit ratio is most favorable. HR's 380-hour investment yielded only 6.7% savings with low discretionary spend (31%), and Finance's cost structure is largely non-discretionary (28%).",
      "C": "Recommend discontinuing ZBB entirely and returning departments to incremental budgeting. The 437-hour average preparation time per ZBB department represents $87,000-$131,000 in manager salary cost, absorbing 30-40% of the gross savings. The net benefit is too small to justify disrupting annual budget processes.",
      "D": "Recommend retaining ZBB only for Facilities Management (highest savings at 17.7%) and returning Radiology and HR to incremental budgeting. Facilities' savings of $640,000 clearly justify the investment, but Radiology's $330,000 savings on 420 hours is borderline, and HR's $130,000 savings does not justify 380 hours of manager time."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The evidence supports targeted ZBB expansion to the departments with the strongest cost-benefit profile (Choice B). The evaluation framework requires weighing three factors for each department: (1) discretionary spending percentage — ZBB is most effective when a high proportion of costs can be challenged; (2) cost growth trajectory under the current method; and (3) implementation cost relative to savings. IT Services (72% discretionary, +6.6% growth) and Marketing (63% discretionary, +6.0% growth) have the highest discretionary percentages among non-ZBB departments and are growing costs under incremental budgeting — precisely the profile where requiring managers to justify every dollar from a zero base produces the greatest savings. The ZBB pilot departments tell a nuanced story: Radiology (58% discretionary, -11.6%) and Facilities (64% discretionary, -17.7%) achieved meaningful savings that justify the process investment. HR (31% discretionary, -6.7%) spent nearly as many hours as Radiology (380 vs. 420) for one-third the dollar savings — its cost structure is largely headcount-driven with limited discretionary spending to challenge. Finance (28% discretionary) has the least flexible cost base — most costs are regulatory, compliance, and transaction-processing activities that must be performed regardless. Under CMA Part 1 budgeting principles, ZBB is not an all-or-nothing decision. It is most appropriate for departments with high discretionary spending and rapid cost growth, where prior-year spending levels are least likely to reflect genuinely required future spending. The correct recommendation targets ZBB where the cost-benefit ratio is strongest and retains incremental budgeting where the cost structure makes ZBB's process burden disproportionate to achievable savings. A common exam trap is treating ZBB success in high-discretionary departments as evidence for enterprise-wide adoption — the methodology's value depends on the nature of each department's costs, not on aggregate pilot results.",
