    "ExplanationWrongA": "Enterprise-wide ZBB adoption treats the pilot results as uniformly positive when the department-level evidence shows significant variation in cost-benefit ratios. HR achieved only 6.7% savings despite investing 380 manager hours — a net benefit of approximately $65,000 after accounting for preparation time cost, which is marginal. Finance has only 28% discretionary spending, meaning 72% of its costs are driven by non-negotiable activities (regulatory reporting, transaction processing, audit support). Applying ZBB to a department with predominantly non-discretionary costs adds significant process burden — managers spending weeks justifying activities they have no authority to eliminate — with minimal savings potential. The correct analysis disaggregates the pilot results by department rather than extrapolating from an aggregate average. Enterprise-wide adoption also ignores that two of the three pilot departments had above-average discretionary spending — the pilot was not a random sample of all support functions.",
