    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "DEMAND PLANNING MEMO — Kestrel Appliance Components\n\nTO: Marcus Delgado, Operations Analyst\nFROM: Demand Analysis Team\nRE: Q3 2026 Forecast Error — Decomposition Analysis\n\nKestrel manufactures refrigerator compressor units for appliance OEMs. The Q3 2026 demand forecast missed actual unit sales by 3,520 units (22.1% error), representing $387,200 in unplanned revenue shortfall. The S&OP meeting is Wednesday, and the VP of Operations needs a root-cause analysis. The team has decomposed the 24-month demand history (Q1 2025 through Q4 2026) into three time-series components:\n\nComponent 1 — Trend: Underlying long-term direction of demand.\n- Monthly trend rate: +2.8% per month (compound).\n- 24-month cumulative trend: demand baseline increased from 12,400 to approximately 15,200 units/month.\n- Q3 forecast error attributable to trend model error: 23%.\n\nComponent 2 — Seasonal: Repeating pattern within each year.\n- Seasonal index: Q1 = 0.88, Q2 = 0.96, Q3 = 1.12, Q4 = 1.04.\n- Q3 peak reflects air conditioning season driving compressor replacement demand.\n- Q3 forecast error attributable to seasonal model error: 35%.\n\nComponent 3 — Irregular (Random): Unexplained variation not captured by trend or seasonal.\n- Includes one-time events: competitor recall of 22,000 units in August 2026 (temporarily increased Kestrel demand by ~2,400 units), hurricane-related logistics disruption that delayed ~800 units of September shipments.\n- Q3 forecast error attributable to irregular component: 42%.\n\nWhich component accounts for the largest share of the Q3 forecast error, and what does this indicate about the forecasting model's performance and the appropriate management response?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The trend component accounts for the largest share (42%), indicating the model's underlying growth assumption of 2.8% per month is unreliable. The model should be recalibrated using a longer historical window before the Wednesday S&OP meeting.",
      "B": "The seasonal component accounts for the largest share (35%), indicating the Q3 seasonal index of 1.12 understates the true seasonal peak. The index should be recalculated using only Q3 data from the most recent five years.",
      "C": "The irregular component accounts for the largest share (42%), indicating that the largest portion of the forecast miss was driven by one-time events (competitor recall and hurricane disruption) that no time-series model can reliably predict. The model's trend and seasonal components performed reasonably — the appropriate response is to document the irregular events for the S&OP meeting rather than recalibrate the model.",
      "D": "The trend and seasonal components combined account for 58% of the error, which exceeds the irregular component's 42%. This indicates the forecasting model itself is the primary source of inaccuracy and should be replaced with a causal regression model that includes external variables such as competitor actions and weather patterns."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The irregular component accounts for the largest share (42%) of the Q3 2026 forecast error. This is diagnostically significant because the irregular component captures one-time, unpredictable events — the competitor recall creating 2,400 units of unexpected demand and the hurricane disrupting 800 units of September shipments. No forecasting model — whether time-series, causal, or judgment-based — can predict competitor recalls or hurricanes. Under CMA Part 1, time-series decomposition separates forecast error into trend, seasonal, and irregular components precisely so management can distinguish between model inadequacy (which requires corrective action) and random variation (which requires acknowledgment and contingency planning). The 23% trend error and 35% seasonal error are within acceptable ranges for appliance-component demand forecasting and do not individually indicate model failure. The appropriate response is not to recalibrate the model (Choices A and B) or replace it (Choice D), but to document the irregular events so that when the S&OP team reviews the 22.1% forecast error, they understand that the model's structural components are performing adequately and the Q3 miss was primarily driven by unforeseeable external shocks. A common exam trap is combining trend and seasonal errors into a single 'model failure' metric — these components represent fundamentally different sources of variation and are not additive in a diagnostic sense.",
