    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "CFO BUSINESS CASE — Apex Optics\n\nTO: James Okonkwo, CFO\nFROM: Budget Transformation Office\nRE: ZBB Implementation Decision for FY2028\n\nApex manufactures precision optical lenses for medical imaging equipment. The company has used incremental budgeting for 15 years — each department's budget is last year's actual spending plus a 4% across-the-board increase. The Board approved a corporate cost-reduction target of $380,000 for FY2028. The status quo incremental budgeting approach has produced average annual cost growth of 5.7% across all support functions over the last three fiscal years. The Budget Transformation Office has evaluated a proposal to implement zero-based budgeting (ZBB) for all six support departments starting FY2028. Key proposal data:\n\nZBB Implementation Costs (one-time, FY2027):\n- ZBB software platform and configuration: $62,000\n- External ZBB consultants (training + process design): $74,000\n- Internal staff time (department managers + finance team, 1,240 hours at $52/hour fully loaded): $64,480\n- Total one-time implementation cost: $200,480\n\nProjected First-Year Savings (FY2028, from ZBB pilot at comparable mid-size manufacturers):\n- Marketing: $94,000 (eliminated low-ROI trade show sponsorships, consolidated digital ad vendors)\n- IT Services: $112,000 (eliminated unused software licenses, renegotiated maintenance contracts)\n- Human Resources: $38,000 (streamlined recruiting vendor contracts)\n- Facilities: $68,000 (renegotiated cleaning and security contracts)\n- Finance: $42,000 (automated manual reconciliation processes)\n- Legal: $26,000 (shifted routine contract review to in-house counsel)\n- Total projected first-year savings: $380,000\n- Estimated annual recurring savings: $340,000 (some first-year savings are one-time vendor contract renegotiation gains)\n\nDepartment Readiness Assessment:\n- Marketing: High (manager has prior ZBB experience; 68% of budget is discretionary)\n- IT Services: High (74% discretionary spend; clear vendor contract inventory)\n- HR: Medium (41% discretionary; heavily regulated compliance spending)\n- Facilities: Medium (52% discretionary; several long-term lease obligations)\n- Finance: Low (28% discretionary; statutory reporting requirements consume most budget)\n- Legal: Low (22% discretionary; litigation costs are externally driven)\n\nThe Board has indicated that if ZBB is not adopted, each department will receive a flat 7% budget cut for FY2028 (projected savings: ~$265,000 across all six departments) with no distinction between essential and discretionary spending. Should Apex implement ZBB for FY2028?",
