    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Option B (Net 15) achieves the fastest collection improvement but at the cost of a projected 12% sales decline — a strategic risk that, in distribution businesses with thin margins, typically outweighs the cash-flow benefit. If Northfield's contribution margin is 35%, the annual bottom-line impact of lost sales is $2,649,600 x 35% = $927,360 in lost contribution. The borrowing savings from reducing the collection period to 31 days (approximately $1,400,000 reduction in borrowing = $133,000 interest savings) plus bad debt reduction ($441,600 to $176,640 = $264,960 saved) totals $397,960 — less than half the contribution margin loss. Additionally, the 12% decline assumption may understate reality: in a fragmented contractor supply market, competitors offering Net 30 or Net 45 will aggressively target Northfield's accounts, and the migration may exceed 12% in the first year as contractors test alternative suppliers. Under management accounting, credit policy must be evaluated on its profit impact, not cash-flow impact alone — revenue loss at contribution margin rates is almost always the largest cost of restrictive credit terms.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Factoring at 3.2% ($706,560 annually) is the most expensive option and should be rejected unless Northfield faces a genuine liquidity crisis (which it does not — the borrowing rate is 9.5%, well below the factoring cost). The effective annualized cost of factoring is 3.2% of sales = 3.2% x $22,080,000 = 35.2% of the borrowing cost. More precisely: factoring costs 3.2% of invoice value versus maintaining the current system which costs 2.0% bad debt + (9.5% x average borrowing balance of $2,140,000 / $22,080,000) = 2.0% + 0.92% = 2.92% of sales. The factoring premium is approximately 28 basis points of sales ($61,824 annually) with the primary benefit being risk transfer. Factoring is appropriate when a company cannot access bank credit, when bad debt risk is concentrated in a few accounts, or when the certainty value of immediate cash exceeds the factoring premium. Northfield has access to bank credit at 9.5% and can improve collections with less expensive tools (Option A).",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Maintaining the status quo (Net 30, no discount) is the default option but leaves $146,990+ of identifiable annual savings unrealized. The 53-day average collection period may be 'within industry norms' but the norm itself reflects an industry that has not optimized collections. The key management accounting insight is that a 2% discount is effectively an annualized interest rate of approximately 36.5% (2% for paying 20 days early = 2% / (20/365) = 36.5%) — expensive when viewed as a financing rate. However, the discount is not paid on all sales — only the 45% of customers who take it. And the discount's primary benefit is not financing-cost substitution but behavioral: it shifts the entire collection curve leftward, reducing borrowing across the full portfolio and converting marginal accounts from eventual bad debt to discounted payers. The relevant comparison is total system cost, not the discount's annualized rate in isolation. Under CMA Part 1, credit policy involves tradeoffs between sales growth, cash flow, and bad debt — maintaining the status quo forgoes measurable improvement.",
