    "Stem": "CONTRACT PRICING MEMO — Aerotech Defense Systems\n\nTO: Elena Vargas, Contracts Director\nFROM: Cost Estimating Team\nRE: Sentinel Radar System — Multi-Year Government Contract Bid Strategy\n\nAerotech is bidding on an 8-year government contract to produce 640 Sentinel radar systems (80 units per year). Two competing cost-projection models have been prepared:\n\nModel 1 — Learning Curve (85% cumulative average-time): Focuses exclusively on direct labor hours. Batch 1 averaged 1,200 labor hours per unit at $65/hour ($78,000 labor cost per unit). Under an 85% learning curve, the cumulative average labor hours decline to 626 hours by year 8 (batch 8), producing labor cost of $40,690 per unit. All other costs (materials, overhead, engineering support) are projected at constant per-unit rates based on year-1 actuals: materials $142,000/unit, overhead $88,000/unit, engineering $34,000/unit — totaling $264,000 in non-labor costs that do not decline with volume.\n\nModel 2 — Experience Curve (82% total-cost): Projects that total per-unit cost (labor + materials + overhead + engineering) declines as cumulative production experience accumulates. Batch 1 total cost: $342,000/unit. Under an 82% experience curve, total per-unit cost declines to $179,000 by year 8, reflecting labor improvements plus supplier volume discounts on materials (projected 18% cumulative reduction), overhead absorption improvement (22% reduction), and engineering standardization (30% reduction).\n\nThe government contracting officer has stated that bidders may use either model but must justify the chosen methodology. The difference in year-8 unit cost between the two models exceeds $84,000 per unit — approximately $6.7 million in total contract value across 80 units in year 8 alone. Which model should Aerotech use for its bid, and why?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Use the Learning Curve model because it is the more conservative approach — it projects higher costs in later years and therefore provides a larger cost buffer. Bidding conservatively reduces the risk of a cost overrun on a fixed-price government contract.",
      "B": "Use the Experience Curve model because it captures cost reductions across all cost categories (labor, materials, overhead, engineering support) rather than direct labor alone. The learning curve systematically understates achievable cost reductions by treating $264,000 in non-labor costs as fixed when evidence shows these costs also decline with cumulative volume, supplier relationships, and process maturity.",
      "C": "Average the two models' year-8 projections to produce a blended bid that balances the risk of underbidding (Experience Curve) against the risk of overbidding and losing the contract to a competitor using the more aggressive Experience Curve model.",
      "D": "Use the Learning Curve model for years 1-4 (where the two models produce similar projections) and switch to the Experience Curve model for years 5-8 (where the divergence becomes material), because learning effects dominate early production while broader experience effects accumulate in later years."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The Experience Curve model is the more complete and appropriate cost-projection methodology for an 8-year production contract (Choice B). The fundamental limitation of the learning curve is scope: it captures only direct labor improvement while treating all other costs as invariant to cumulative production experience. For Aerotech's Sentinel system, $264,000 of the $342,000 year-1 unit cost (77%) is non-labor — materials, overhead, and engineering support. Assuming these costs remain constant over 8 years of production ignores well-documented cost-reduction mechanisms: suppliers offer volume discounts as cumulative orders grow (materials), fixed overhead is absorbed over a larger cumulative production base (overhead), and engineering processes standardize as design changes diminish (engineering support). The experience curve, originating from Boston Consulting Group research, captures total cost reduction — the combined effect of labor learning, supplier scale economies, process improvement, and technological advancement — as cumulative volume increases. Under CMA Part 1, the learning curve is a narrower concept: it describes the reduction in direct labor hours per unit as workers gain experience with a specific task. The experience curve is broader: it describes the decline in total unit cost as cumulative production experience accumulates across the entire value chain. For a multi-year contract where non-labor costs represent the majority of total cost, restricting the projection methodology to labor-only learning systematically understates the total cost reduction achievable and produces a non-competitive bid.",
