    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "CONTROLLER'S MEMO — Meridian Engineered Products\n\nTO: Sarah Kessler, Controller\nFROM: Cost Accounting Department\nRE: FY2027 Fixed Overhead Rate — Capacity Measure Selection\n\nMeridian manufactures precision hydraulic components. The cost accounting team must select a capacity measure for calculating the FY2027 fixed overhead application rate. Three capacity measures have been proposed:\n\nCapacity Measure | Machine Hours | Fixed Overhead Rate/Hour | Rationale\nIdeal (Theoretical) Capacity | 62,000 hours | $14.52/hr ($900,000 / 62,000) | Assumes 24/7 operation with zero downtime, no maintenance, no holidays — maximum output if everything runs perfectly.\nPractical Capacity | 52,000 hours | $17.31/hr ($900,000 / 52,000) | Assumes normal operating conditions: 8,000 hours for scheduled maintenance, 1,500 hours for holidays, 500 hours for normal startup/shutdown.\nNormal (Budgeted) Capacity | 44,000 hours | $20.45/hr ($900,000 / 44,000) | Based on average expected demand over the next 3-5 years. Current year demand is 43,000 hours; the 5-year average is 44,000.\n\nFixed manufacturing overhead is budgeted at $900,000. The VP of Sales argues for ideal capacity rates (lowest overhead per unit = most competitive pricing). The Production Manager argues for practical capacity (reflects what the plant can realistically produce). The Controller must consider implications for product costing, capacity management, and the production volume variance. Which capacity measure should Meridian select for its FY2027 fixed overhead application rate, and what is the primary justification?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Select ideal capacity (62,000 hours, $14.52/hr). The lowest overhead rate per unit makes Meridian's products most competitive on price. Idle capacity costs will be captured as a production volume variance, which is reported separately and does not affect inventory valuation.",
      "B": "Select practical capacity (52,000 hours, $17.31/hr). This measure excludes unavoidable downtime (maintenance, holidays) but includes available production time, producing an overhead rate that reflects the cost of capacity made available for production. The resulting production volume variance highlights the cost of unused capacity — a signal management can act on to increase sales volume, reduce capacity, or find alternative uses.",
      "C": "Select normal capacity (44,000 hours, $20.45/hr). This measure smoothes cyclical demand fluctuations over a multi-year average, producing the most stable overhead rate for inventory valuation and the smallest production volume variance. Stability in reported product costs is the primary objective of overhead rate selection.",
      "D": "Select ideal capacity for external financial reporting (ASC 330 requires theoretical capacity for GAAP inventory valuation) and practical capacity for internal management reporting. Dual-rate systems satisfy both GAAP compliance and operational decision-making needs."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Practical capacity is the most appropriate measure for Meridian's fixed overhead application rate (Choice B). Under CMA Part 1 cost management principles, practical capacity provides the best combination of: (1) realistic product costing — the overhead rate of $17.31/hr reflects the cost of capacity actually made available for production, excluding only unavoidable downtime; (2) meaningful capacity management signals — the production volume variance (also called the fixed overhead production-volume variance) equals (actual hours - denominator hours) x fixed overhead rate, and when the denominator is practical capacity, the unfavorable variance directly measures the cost of unused available capacity — a signal management can use to pursue additional sales, reduce capacity, or find alternative uses for idle resources; (3) reasonable inventory valuation — practical capacity avoids the distortion of ideal capacity (which understates product costs by spreading overhead over unattainable volume) while avoiding the cyclical instability of normal capacity (which changes as multi-year demand averages shift). The selection of capacity measure is both a product-costing decision (affecting inventory values and COGS) and a capacity-management decision (affecting what variances signal to management). Practical capacity is generally preferred because it anchors overhead rates to the capacity the company has chosen to maintain, making the cost of unused capacity transparent rather than buried in inflated product costs (ideal capacity) or smoothed away (normal capacity).",
