    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Overland Industries' controller reviews the six-quarter sales forecasting track record and finds that actual sales have exceeded forecasts by 8%, 12%, 9%, 14%, 11%, and 10% respectively. The VP of Sales argues that the consistent over-performance demonstrates the sales team's effectiveness and that forecasts should remain unchanged to maintain motivation. The production manager reports frequent overtime costs and expedited raw material orders due to the consistent under-forecasting. Which recommendation should the controller present to the CFO?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Maintain the current forecasting methodology — consistent over-performance validates the sales team's effort and should not be penalized with forecast adjustments",
      "B": "Adjust the forecasting model upward by the average historical bias of 10.7% and implement quarterly forecast accuracy reviews with accountability for both under-forecasting and over-forecasting",
      "C": "Switch entirely to a qualitative forecasting approach based on sales team judgment — the current quantitative model is demonstrably unreliable given six consecutive periods of error",
      "D": "Reduce the forecast to 50% of expected sales so production can consistently exceed expectations — this eliminates overtime costs and expediting fees"
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The controller should recommend correcting the systematic upward bias in the forecasting model and implementing ongoing accuracy monitoring. The data shows a clear, persistent pattern: actual sales exceed forecasts by an average of 10.7% with a range of 8-14% and zero periods of under-performance. This is textbook systematic bias — the model is structurally underestimating sales, generating downstream operational costs in the form of overtime premiums and expedited raw material orders. The recommendation has two components: (1) Model correction — adjusting upward by the average historical bias (10.7%) aligns the forecast with actual performance. This is not penalizing the sales team; it is making the forecast useful for production planning, inventory management, and cash flow projection. (2) Governance — quarterly forecast accuracy reviews with bidirectional accountability (penalizing both under- and over-forecasting) prevent gaming. If only under-forecasting is penalized, managers inflate forecasts; if only over-forecasting is penalized (as the VP of Sales implicitly argues), forecasts become useless for operational planning. A forecasting model that is consistently wrong in one direction is not performing adequately regardless of whether the error is favorable or unfavorable from a sales perspective. Business interpretation: a forecast exists to enable planning decisions — production schedules, material purchases, staffing levels. A biased forecast generates real costs: overtime, expediting fees, and production inefficiencies. The VP of Sales' argument confuses performance evaluation (the sales team's effectiveness) with forecasting accuracy (the model's ability to predict outcomes). A common exam trap is to treat consistent over-performance as a positive signal rather than evidence of a broken forecasting process.",
