    "ExplanationWrongA": "Six consecutive quarters of error in the same direction is the opposite of random — it is a clear, persistent pattern. Random error would fluctuate above and below actual results with no consistent direction. This directional consistency across multiple periods is the textbook definition of bias, requiring model adjustment, not dismissal as chance. Maintaining the current methodology would perpetuate the operational costs identified by the production manager: recurring overtime, expedited raw material orders, and production inefficiencies. The VP of Sales' argument confuses sales team performance evaluation with forecasting accuracy — a biased forecast generates real operational costs regardless of whether the sales team is performing well.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Switching entirely to qualitative judgment-based forecasting would represent a regression in methodology. Qualitative approaches (sales force composites, executive opinion, Delphi method) are appropriate when historical data is unavailable or unreliable — not when a quantitative model has produced six periods of consistently biased but stable results. The model is not 'unreliable' — it is biased, which is a different problem. Bias is correctable through statistical adjustment, whereas qualitative methods introduce new error sources: individual optimism or pessimism bias, anchoring to recent events, and inconsistency between forecasters. A biased but consistent quantitative model is preferable to an unbiased but inconsistent qualitative one. A candidate selecting this option confuses the presence of error with the failure of the methodology — the correct fix is to adjust the model, not abandon quantitative methods.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Deliberately under-forecasting by 50% would eliminate overtime costs by ensuring production always exceeds expectations, but this recommendation is fundamentally unsound. A forecast set at 50% of expected sales would generate massive excess inventory, idle capacity, and working capital tied up in unsold goods — all far more costly than overtime premiums. More importantly, this approach destroys the purpose of forecasting: enabling rational resource planning. If the organization knows the forecast is deliberately wrong, no downstream decision (production scheduling, material purchasing, cash flow planning, performance evaluation) can rely on it. A candidate selecting this option is addressing a symptom (overtime costs) with a solution that creates larger problems than it solves — the correct approach is to fix the forecast model, not game it.",
