    "ExplanationCorrect": "Risk assessment is the second component of the COSO Internal Control--Integrated Framework, following the control environment. COSO Principle 6 requires management to specify objectives with sufficient clarity to enable the identification and assessment of risks relating to those objectives. When implementing a new revenue system, the organization must first identify the risks the system introduces to financial reporting, operations, and compliance objectives, then analyze their likelihood and impact before designing control responses. This aligns with the COSO ERM framework's emphasis that risk assessment precedes risk response. Business interpretation: a new revenue system may introduce risks such as unauthorized access, data integrity failures, revenue leakage, or non-compliance with revenue recognition standards; management must understand these risks to design effective controls. Exam trap: candidates sometimes jump directly to control activities (such as system access restrictions) without first performing the risk assessment step. The proper sequence in COSO is risk assessment first, then control activities.",
