    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Three-way matching is a preventive control activity under COSO Principle 10 in the procure-to-pay cycle. It requires that the purchase order (what was ordered), receiving report (what was received), and vendor invoice (what the vendor is billing) are compared and found consistent before payment is approved. Discrepancies in quantity, price, or specifications are investigated and resolved before disbursement. This control prevents overpayment, duplicate payment, and payment for goods not received. Business interpretation: three-way matching is one of the most fundamental controls in the expenditure cycle because it creates an independent check at the point of payment. It should be performed by accounts payable personnel who are separate from purchasing and receiving functions to maintain segregation of duties. Common exam trap: some candidates confuse three-way matching with two-way matching (PO to invoice only). Two-way matching is less rigorous and does not verify receipt of goods, making it less effective at preventing payment for unordered or unreceived items.",
