    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the COSO Internal Control — Integrated Framework, the internal audit function plays a critical role in the monitoring component. COSO Principle 16 requires the organization to conduct ongoing and/or separate evaluations of internal control effectiveness, and internal audit is a primary mechanism for performing these evaluations. To be effective, internal audit must maintain organizational independence and objectivity. The IIA International Standards for the Professional Practice of Internal Auditing and COSO both emphasize that the chief audit executive should report functionally to the board or audit committee, not to management whose activities are being audited. This reporting relationship ensures that internal audit can objectively evaluate controls across all levels of the organization, including senior management decisions, without fear of retaliation or undue influence. Administrative reporting to management (for budgeting and logistics) is acceptable, but functional reporting for audit scope, findings, and performance evaluation must reside with the board or audit committee. COSO Principle 2 further supports this by requiring the board to demonstrate independence from management and exercise oversight of the internal control system, and internal audit serves as a key resource enabling that oversight.",
