    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "INTERNAL AUDIT FINDING — Keystone Hospital Supply\n\nTO: Audit Committee Chair\nFROM: Sophia Chen, Director of Internal Audit\nRE: Q2 Control Exception — Invoice Approval Workflow\n\nDuring the Q2 internal audit, we identified 148 invoice approval exceptions across Keystone's four regional procurement offices. The exceptions are concentrated in the Southwest region (82 of 148, or 55%), despite the Southwest region processing only 22% of total invoice volume. Further analysis revealed within the Southwest region, 64 of the 82 exceptions (78%) were associated with only two of the region's six approval managers. The other four approval managers in the Southwest region had exception rates consistent with the company average.\n\nThe exceptions fall into three categories: (1) 52 invoices exceeding the approver's delegated authority limit by more than $10,000 without documented escalation; (2) 24 invoices approved by the same person who entered the invoice into the system — a segregation-of-duties conflict; and (3) 6 invoices sent to a manager who had transferred to a different department and whose approval credentials should have been revoked under the access recertification policy.\n\nAll 148 invoices were eventually paid, and no fraudulent disbursements have been detected. The Procurement Director argues the exceptions are administrative processing errors with no financial impact and recommends no further action. Which recommendation should the Director of Internal Audit present to the Audit Committee?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Accept the Procurement Director's assessment and close the finding as immaterial because all 148 invoices were eventually paid and no fraudulent disbursements resulted from the exceptions.",
      "B": "Recommend that the two high-exception approval managers in the Southwest region attend remedial training on invoice approval procedures, and monitor the Southwest region's exception rate in Q3 to see if it declines.",
      "C": "Recommend that Keystone eliminate the invoice approval control for the Southwest region and implement a post-payment statistical sampling review program, because the high exception rate indicates the approval process is not functioning as designed and consumes resources without adding compensating value.",
      "D": "Recommend a targeted root-cause investigation into why the Southwest region has a disproportionate concentration of exceptions — specifically why two of six approval managers account for 78% of the region's exceptions, why approvals exceed delegated authority limits, and why the access recertification policy failed to revoke a transferred manager's credentials — and require a written remediation plan with Q3 monitoring metrics."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 17, the organization evaluates and communicates internal control deficiencies in a timely manner to parties responsible for taking corrective action, including senior management and the board of directors as appropriate. The Keystone finding presents a pattern that demands root-cause analysis rather than surface-level remediation. Three distinct control deficiencies exist: (1) Delegated authority limits are bypassed in 52 of 148 cases, suggesting either the limits are improperly calibrated or the approval workflow allows override without detection; (2) Segregation of duties is violated in 24 cases where the same person enters and approves invoices — a fundamental control principle under COSO Principle 10 that prevents an individual from both perpetrating and concealing errors or irregularities; (3) The access recertification policy failed, allowing a transferred manager to retain approval credentials — this is both a design deficiency (the recertification process did not trigger on transfer) and an operating deficiency (the credentials were not revoked). The fact that no fraud has been detected is not evidence that the control environment is adequate — it may mean only that existing monitoring is insufficient to detect problems. The concentration pattern (82 of 148 exceptions in one region; 64 of those 82 tied to two managers) is a classic red flag in internal auditing. Concentration of exceptions in specific individuals or locations frequently signals either inadequate training, excessive workload, or intentional circumvention — each requiring a different investigative approach. The recommendation for a targeted root-cause investigation addresses the underlying drivers of the pattern rather than treating the observable symptoms. The Q3 monitoring metrics ensure the remediation is verified for effectiveness, consistent with COSO Principle 16's requirement that ongoing monitoring confirms controls operate as designed. A common exam trap is treating all control exceptions as equivalent routine errors — the CMA exam expects candidates to distinguish between random, isolated exceptions and patterned, concentrated exceptions that signal systematic control failures requiring investigative response.",
