    "ExplanationWrongA": "Bank reconciliation is a detective control, not a transaction-processing function. Staff B's reconciliation role is designed to independently verify that cash transactions recorded in the bank statement match the company's records — it does not initiate, authorize, or execute disbursements. The fact that Staff B also investigates discrepancies actually strengthens the control environment because investigation follows naturally from detection: the same person who identifies an unusual item is best positioned to trace its source. The auditor's finding — a $34,000 payment with no purchase order, receiving report, or vendor record — could not have been prevented or detected by Staff B because the payment was already executed by Staff A before any reconciliation occurred. Placing blame on the detective control rather than on the collapsed authorization-recording-custody combination in Staff A's role misidentifies the root cause of the control failure.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "A four-person department can achieve adequate — not perfect, but adequate — segregation of duties through deliberate assignment of incompatible functions. The assertion that adding a fifth person is the only solution ignores the possibility of redistributing existing duties among the current four staff members. The minimum viable change is to break the most dangerous combination (Staff A's triple role) by moving one function — invoice approval — to another staff member. After this single reassignment, Staff A retains invoice entry and payment processing (recording plus custody, a residual risk but manageable when paired with independent authorization). Many small and mid-size organizations operate with limited accounting staff; COSO explicitly acknowledges that segregation of duties can be achieved through compensating controls and management oversight when perfect structural separation is impractical. Hiring additional staff is one option, but it is not the only — or necessarily the best — remediation.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Journal entry preparation and general ledger posting are typically performed by the same individual in organizations of all sizes and do not represent a segregation of duties violation under COSO Principle 10. Both functions fall within the recording category: the journal entry is the initial record of a transaction, and posting to the general ledger is the summarization of that same record. Unlike Staff A's combination — which spans recording, authorization, and custody across the entire vendor disbursement cycle — Staff C's duties are confined to a single functional category. The auditor's finding involved a payment executed without supporting documentation, which is a custody and authorization failure traceable to Staff A, not a recording error by Staff C. There is no indication in the scenario that Staff C posted any incorrect entries; the transaction was recorded as directed by Staff A, and the general ledger accurately reflected the disbursement.",
