    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "The gross-to-net calculation engine is a processing control, not an input validation control. Its function is arithmetic: it multiplies hours by pay rate, applies tax withholding formulas, subtracts benefit deductions, and computes net pay. It does not maintain or reference each employee's standard schedule, nor does it perform any comparison of entered hours to expected hours. Assigning input-validation capabilities to a calculation engine confuses the role of processing controls (which operate on data already admitted into the system) with the role of input controls (which gate data before it enters processing). If an employee's standard schedule is 40 hours but 120 hours are entered, the gross-to-net engine will faithfully compute 120 hours of gross pay, apply all applicable deductions, and produce a net pay amount — all correctly from a computational standpoint. The absence of any alert from Controls 2, 3, and 4 during the three-month period confirms that these processing and output controls did not and cannot detect unreasonable input values.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "The 15% tolerance check compares total company-wide payroll dollars for the current biweekly period against the prior period's total — it operates at the aggregate level, not at the individual employee level. This is a critical distinction. If 5,000 employees have a combined payroll of approximately $12 million per period, a single employee's fraudulent $5,000 overpayment represents roughly 0.04% of the total — well within the 15% band. Even several simultaneous overpayments could remain below the threshold if offset by employees on leave, reduced overtime, or normal period-to-period fluctuations in hours worked and overtime premiums. A control that only triggers when the company-wide variance exceeds 15% cannot reliably detect individual-level fraud. This misconception treats an aggregate reasonableness check as if it were an employee-by-employee audit, which it structurally is not.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "The $2.5 million exception-report threshold is an output control, not a per-transaction or per-employee control. For a company with 5,000 employees and a biweekly payroll that likely runs $10-14 million, an output report that only prints when the total exceeds $2.5 million would trigger in virtually every payroll run under normal conditions — which suggests the threshold is either misdescribed in this option or would already be firing constantly. More importantly, even if the threshold were high enough to serve as a genuine exception indicator, it operates on the aggregate. A handful of individual overpayments — say, five employees each overpaid by $4,000, totaling $20,000 — represents 0.15-0.20% of a $10-14 million payroll. This amount would not cause aggregate payroll to exceed a properly calibrated threshold by a meaningful margin. Output controls summarize and present processed data; they cannot reconstruct whether the original input data was valid.",
