    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Ascent Corp's accounts payable department processes approximately 8,000 vendor invoices monthly. The department's exception report applies three detection rules: (1) flag any invoice exceeding $10,000 for manager approval, (2) flag duplicate invoice numbers, and (3) flag any change to the vendor master file. Monthly, the exception report generates 900 to 1,200 flags. The AP manager estimates that approximately 850 of these flags are false positives — nearly all from the $10,000 threshold rule, which triggers on recurring monthly payments for rent, utilities, equipment leases, and raw material purchases from long-established vendors. The AP team spends roughly 40 staff-hours per month reviewing flagged items. In the most recent quarter, three duplicate payments totaling $47,000 went undetected because the genuine duplicate-invoice flags were buried among the high volume of threshold-based false positives and overlooked during review. Analyze the exception report design: what is the primary deficiency, and what parameter adjustment would most effectively reduce false positives while preserving detection of genuine exceptions?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The duplicate invoice detection rule is the primary deficiency because it failed to prevent three duplicate payments totaling $47,000; the rule's matching logic needs to be tightened to catch near-matches in addition to exact invoice-number matches.",
      "B": "The $10,000 threshold is calibrated too low relative to Ascent's normal invoice profile, generating approximately 850 false positives per month from recurring legitimate payments. The primary fix is to raise the threshold to a risk-calibrated level and create a whitelist of approved recurring vendors whose payments are exempt from the threshold rule.",
      "C": "The vendor master file change flag is the primary deficiency because unauthorized vendor changes could enable fraudulent payments; this rule should be elevated to trigger an immediate system block rather than appearing on a periodic exception report.",
      "D": "A monthly volume of 900 to 1,200 exception flags is an acceptable level for a department processing 8,000 invoices, representing an 11% to 15% exception rate that is consistent with industry benchmarks for accounts payable monitoring."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under COSO Principle 13 (Obtains, Generates, and Uses Relevant, Quality Information), control information must be relevant, timely, and of sufficient quality to support the functioning of internal control. The primary deficiency in Ascent Corp's exception report is not the presence of controls but their calibration. The $10,000 threshold was likely set without reference to Ascent's actual invoice population. When a single parameter generates approximately 850 of 900-1,200 total flags — meaning 71% to 94% of all exceptions are false positives from one rule — the threshold is objectively too low. The consequence, evidenced by the three missed duplicate payments, is alert fatigue: when reviewers are inundated with irrelevant flags, they become desensitized and genuine exceptions are overlooked. The correct remediation has two components. First, raise the dollar threshold to a level calibrated to Ascent's actual invoice distribution — for example, by analyzing the invoice population to identify the natural breakpoint above which invoices are genuinely anomalous. Second, implement a whitelist of approved recurring vendors (landlord, utility companies, equipment lessors, key raw material suppliers) whose invoices are excluded from the threshold rule because the purchasing relationship is governed by long-term contracts and separate approval processes. This preserves the threshold rule for non-recurring or ad-hoc vendor payments where it provides genuine detective value, while eliminating the false positives that currently overwhelm the review process.",
