    "ExplanationWrongA": "The duplicate invoice detection rule is not the deficiency — it is functioning exactly as designed. The rule successfully flagged the three duplicate payments; the problem is that those flags were lost in a sea of 850 false positives from the threshold rule, not that the duplicate detection logic was inadequate. Tightening the matching logic to catch near-matches might be a useful enhancement, but it does not address the root cause of the control failure: the review process is overwhelmed by irrelevant threshold-based flags, not by an insufficient number of duplicate detections. If the duplicate detection rule is already catching the exact matches and the only issue is that reviewers cannot find them among the noise, the solution is to reduce the noise, not to generate more flags from the same rule. This option misidentifies the symptom (missed duplicates) as the deficiency rather than tracing it to its cause (threshold-generated alert fatigue).",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "The vendor master file change flag addresses a different risk — unauthorized modifications to the approved vendor list — and there is no evidence in the scenario that this rule is malfunctioning or that vendor master file changes contributed to the three missed duplicate payments. Elevating this rule to an immediate system block rather than a periodic report flag could be a worthwhile control enhancement, but it does not solve the problem described in the scenario: the duplicate payment flags are being overlooked because reviewers are overwhelmed by threshold-based false positives. Changing the vendor master file rule leaves the $10,000 threshold rule generating 850 false positives per month unchanged, which means the alert-fatigue problem persists and genuine duplicates will continue to be missed. This option addresses a valid control consideration but it is not responsive to the primary deficiency identified in the scenario analysis.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "An 11%-15% exception rate is not inherently problematic in all contexts, but the critical distinction here is the false-positive rate, not the raw exception rate. When approximately 850 of 900-1,200 flags are false positives — meaning 71%-94% of all flagged items require no action — the exception report is not generating useful information; it is generating noise. Industry benchmarks for exception reporting focus on the signal-to-noise ratio and the actionable-exception rate, not on the raw percentage of transactions flagged. A well-calibrated exception report should produce a manageable volume of genuinely actionable items, ideally with a false-positive rate below 20%-30%. Ascent Corp's false-positive rate of 71%-94% for a single rule indicates that the rule parameters are not calibrated to the organization's actual transaction profile. The three missed duplicate payments totaling $47,000 provide concrete evidence that the current volume is not acceptable: it directly caused a control failure with financial consequences.",
