    "ExplanationWrongA": "COSO Principle 6 (Specifies Suitable Objectives) addresses the establishment of clear objectives that support the identification and assessment of risks. It is a foundational principle concerned with objective-setting as a precondition to risk assessment and control design — it does not govern how those objectives and controls are communicated to personnel. Northfield Industries did establish objectives (the control framework itself represents objective-setting), and management did design controls (the 45-page manual documents them). The failure is not in the absence of objectives but in the failure to communicate them effectively to the people who must execute the controls. The survey results — 78% unable to identify personal control responsibilities, 62% never trained — are indicators of a communication breakdown, not an objective-setting failure. Confusing Principle 6 (objective-setting within the control environment and risk assessment components) with Principle 14 (communication within the information and communication component) conflates two distinct COSO principles operating in different components of the framework.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Eighteen months is more than sufficient time for a control framework to be absorbed by an organization of Northfield Industries' size. The survey results are not a reflection of insufficient time — they are direct evidence of a communication failure. Three-quarters of employees cannot name a single control responsibility a year and a half after implementation, and nearly two-thirds have never received any training whatsoever. A 45-page manual distributed once via email to department heads, with no acknowledgment mechanism, no training sessions, and no follow-up, is not adequate communication under any interpretation of COSO Principle 14. The principle explicitly requires that communication enables personnel to understand internal control responsibilities; the survey demonstrates the opposite. Furthermore, management's approach places the entire communication burden on department heads, who may lack the time, expertise, or incentive to effectively cascade control information to their teams — and management performed no verification to determine whether cascading occurred. Treating demonstrable communication failure as a timing issue rather than a process deficiency would delay remediation indefinitely while the control gap persists.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "COSO Principle 16 (Conducts Ongoing and/or Separate Evaluations) addresses the monitoring component of internal control — the process of assessing whether controls are present and functioning over time. While it is true that Northfield Industries' management failed to monitor whether employees understood their control responsibilities (no acknowledgment, no follow-up assessment), the root cause is more fundamental: the communication itself was deficient. The internal audit survey is actually an example of a separate evaluation (a Principle 16 activity) functioning correctly — it identified the gap. But the gap it identified is a communication failure under Principle 14, not a monitoring failure under Principle 16. Monitoring can detect that communication was ineffective, but the deficiency is in the communication design, not in the monitoring process. Effective monitoring would not have prevented the communication gap from existing; effective communication design would have. This option correctly identifies a secondary deficiency but misidentifies it as the primary one.",
