    "ExplanationWrongA": "Accepting the provider's statement at face value violates COSO Principle 15's requirement that external communication be evaluated for sufficiency. A SOC 1 Type 2 report with an identified exception is not a clean opinion — it is a qualified or adverse conclusion on the control for the affected period. The provider's unverified assertion, without evidence, does not satisfy management's responsibility to obtain sufficient information about whether the deficiency was actually corrected. Furthermore, COSO Principle 10 requires control activities that mitigate identified risks; accepting an unverified claim of remediation leaves Greenway exposed to the same control weakness for any billing adjustments and write-offs processed since the exception period. The independence of the service auditor does not excuse management from its own obligation to evaluate the impact on Greenway's financial reporting and to ensure that risks to the integrity of accounts receivable are addressed through evidence-based follow-up rather than reliance on an unsupported statement from the service provider.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Immediate contract termination is disproportionate to a single quarterly exception and is not what COSO Principle 15 contemplates. Service organization relationships involve multi-year contracts with significant transition costs, data migration requirements, and operational disruption risks. A single control exception covering a three-month period, while requiring investigation, does not necessarily indicate a pervasive control failure — the SOC report itself would describe the exception's severity and whether it represented a material weakness or a less severe deficiency. Furthermore, termination does not resolve the outstanding question of whether billing adjustments and write-offs during the affected period were processed correctly; those receivables remain on Greenway's books regardless of the provider relationship. The appropriate response under COSO is to evaluate the severity, obtain remediation evidence, and implement compensating controls — not to abandon the relationship without assessment. Termination without analysis substitutes an operational decision for a control evaluation, addressing neither the root cause nor the financial reporting impact.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Expanding the financial statement audit to test 100% of receivables conflates management's responsibility for internal control with the external auditor's independent attest role. Under COSO Principle 15, management — not the external auditor — is responsible for evaluating exceptions identified in service organization reports and determining whether compensating controls are needed. External auditors are not a substitute for management's internal control responsibilities; they provide independent assurance, not ongoing management oversight. Furthermore, auditing 100% of $8.5M in receivables is neither cost-effective nor a permanent solution — it addresses the symptom (potential receivable misstatement) rather than the cause (unremediated segregation-of-duties failure at the service provider). Every subsequent period would require the same expanded audit testing unless the underlying control issue is resolved, making this approach an indefinite, unsustainable use of audit resources. The correct response is for management to obtain remediation evidence from the provider and implement compensating controls at Greenway.",
