    "ExplanationWrongA": "Attributing the failure to COSO Principle 14 (internal communication) misidentifies the root cause. While communication is a component of effective internal control — management should communicate remediation status to relevant parties — the deficiency at Ridgeway is not primarily a communication failure. The core issues are: (1) management self-certified remediation without independent verification (5 of 11 remediations were ineffective), (2) no tracking mechanism existed for in-progress items (3 items with no updates in 8 months), and (3) the CEO believes self-assessment is sufficient. This is a deficiency assessment, tracking, and verification failure under COSO Principle 17, not a communication failure under Principle 14. Issuing a company-wide memo on control compliance — the proposed fix — would not establish independent verification of remediation effectiveness, create a formal tracking system with assigned owners and target dates, or provide the audit committee with reliable information about whether control weaknesses have been resolved. A memo communicates; it does not verify, track, or remediate. Principle 17 specifically requires that the organization track whether deficiencies are remediated on a timely basis — a tracking system, not a memo.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Blaming COSO Principle 10 (control activities) and recommending a full redesign of all 14 controls misunderstands the nature of the deficiency. The original controls may have been appropriately designed but failed due to operating errors (e.g., personnel not following prescribed procedures, inadequate training, workload pressures) that do not require wholesale redesign. More fundamentally, the finding described by internal audit is a monitoring and remediation tracking failure, not a control design failure. COSO Principle 17 requires the organization to evaluate deficiencies and track whether corrective actions are completed on a timely basis. Ridgeway's problem is not that the controls were poorly designed — it is that management declared controls remediated without verifying the remediation's effectiveness (5 of 11 still defective) and had no process for monitoring the progress of in-progress items (3 items with no updates in 8 months). Even the best-designed controls require a functioning remediation tracking and independent verification process to ensure that identified weaknesses are actually corrected — which is precisely what Ridgeway lacks.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Invoking COSO Principle 5 (accountability) and requiring the CEO to personally verify each remediation is both a misdiagnosis of the principle violated and an impractical, non-scalable solution. COSO Principle 5 addresses the board's oversight role and the organization's establishment of structures, reporting lines, and appropriate authorities and responsibilities in pursuit of objectives. While accountability for internal control is important, Principle 5 does not specifically address deficiency remediation tracking or independent verification — those are squarely within COSO Principle 17's scope. Furthermore, requiring the CEO to personally review and sign off on every remediation at a granular level is neither practical for an organization the size of Ridgeway Corp nor an effective control: the CEO relies on a system of internal control — including internal audit, the remediation tracking process, and the audit committee's oversight — rather than performing verification personally. The core deficiency is that Ridgeway's monitoring system lacked independent verification (management self-certified without objective testing). The appropriate fix is to build independent verification into the remediation tracking process — through internal audit validation, not CEO sign-off — which is the structural solution that COSO Principle 17 envisions.",
