    "ExplanationWrongB": "COSO Principle 8 requires the organization to consider the potential for fraud in assessing risks to the achievement of objectives. While fraud risk assessment is important at Crestwood, it identifies risks — it does not prevent them. The question asks which control principle would most directly prevent the combination of responsibilities described. Principle 8 produces awareness of fraud risk; Principle 10 produces the segregation of duties that prevents one person from having the opportunity to perpetrate and conceal fraud. A candidate may select this by confusing assessing fraud risk with designing controls to address that risk.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Replacing manual posting with automated cash application software does not address the segregation-of-duties problem. Even if posting is automated, the same staff accountant would still open the mail containing patient payments and prepare the bank deposit — retaining physical custody of cash. Automation addresses the recordkeeping function but does not segregate custody from recording. Furthermore, no software can fully substitute for the fundamental control principle of separating incompatible duties. At Crestwood, different individuals should handle custody, recording, and reconciliation regardless of the level of automation.",
