    "primaryTheory": "E1",
    "Difficulty": "Difficult",
    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Grandview Enterprises controller Elaine Park has completed the annual internal control assessment and identified four unremediated deficiencies from the prior year's audit. The audit committee has authorized remediation funding of $180,000 for the current fiscal year, which Park must allocate across competing priorities. The four deficiencies are:\n\n1. Procure-to-pay three-way match: System bypasses the purchase order step for 22% of invoices under $5,000 when entered by senior buyers. No dollar loss has been documented, but the auditor estimates $8,000–$15,000 in annual duplicate payments based on peer benchmarks. Remediation estimate: $42,000.\n\n2. Vendor master file access: Four purchasing clerks can create and modify vendor records without secondary approval. Last year, a clerk added a fictitious vendor, submitted $7,400 in false invoices, and was terminated. Controls have not been strengthened since the incident. Remediation estimate: $85,000.\n\n3. Bank reconciliation timing: Reconciliations for the primary operating account are completed 45–60 days after month-end due to staff workload. Cash disbursements average $2.1 million monthly. The external auditor has flagged this as a material weakness for two consecutive years. Remediation estimate: $28,000.\n\n4. Journal entry review: 34% of non-standard journal entries are posted without supervisory review during the first three business days of each month. No fraudulent entries have been found, but the volume of unreviewed entries is rising (~15 entries/month last year, ~22 entries/month this year). Remediation estimate: $25,000.\n\nPark must present her prioritized remediation plan to the audit committee next week. Which deficiency should receive the highest priority, and why?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "The vendor master file access control deficiency, because a known fraud loss has already occurred, failure to remediate signals tolerance of control weakness, and the control addresses the root cause of the prior $7,400 theft — without it, the same scheme can recur indefinitely.",
      "B": "The bank reconciliation timing deficiency, because the external auditor has classified it as a material weakness for two consecutive years, cash disbursements of $2.1 million monthly represent the largest dollar exposure, and an adverse SOX 404 opinion is imminent if remediation is delayed further.",
      "C": "The procure-to-pay three-way match bypass, because 22% of invoices bypass controls systematically, the estimated $8,000–$15,000 annual loss is recurring and predictable, and automated three-way matching is the most cost-effective control per dollar of risk mitigated.",
      "D": "The journal entry review deficiency, because the volume of unreviewed entries is growing (47% increase year-over-year), journal entries are the highest-risk transaction category for financial statement fraud, and the remediation cost is the lowest at $25,000 — enabling Park to fund a second deficiency within the $180,000 budget."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The vendor master file access control deficiency must receive the highest priority. Under COSO Principle 17 (Ongoing and Separate Evaluations), management must evaluate and prioritize control deficiencies based on severity, likelihood, and impact. The vendor master file deficiency is the only one of the four that has already resulted in a documented control failure with financial loss ($7,400 in confirmed fraud). A known-exploited control weakness presents the highest residual risk because the threat actor's method is proven and the vulnerability remains open — the same scheme can be executed again at any time by any of the four clerks who retain unrestricted vendor creation access. While the bank reconciliation timing deficiency carries a material weakness classification and larger dollar exposure, its risk profile is different: delayed reconciliations create detection lag, not theft opportunity. The vendor master file deficiency enables direct asset misappropriation that has already succeeded once. The audit committee's fiduciary responsibility under COSO's control environment principle requires demonstrating that management responds decisively to known fraud — failure to prioritize this deficiency signals that internal control breaches carry no consequences, which undermines the entire control environment. The $85,000 remediation cost, while the highest of the four, consumes less than half the authorized $180,000 budget and leaves $95,000 available to address the bank reconciliation and journal entry deficiencies in combination ($28,000 + $25,000 = $53,000). A common exam trap is to automatically prioritize the deficiency with the largest dollar exposure or the external auditor's material weakness label, rather than evaluating the unique risk characteristic of a proven-exploited control gap.",
