    "ExplanationWrongA": "Reducing audit committee meetings to annually would decrease oversight frequency, weakening governance rather than strengthening it. Under SOX and stock exchange listing standards, quarterly meetings are the minimum expected frequency — audit committees of publicly traded companies typically meet four to six times per year. SignalPoint's quarterly meeting schedule is already at the minimum; the issue is not the quantity of meetings but their quality and independence. A recommendation that reduces meeting frequency addresses a non-existent problem while ignoring the actual governance deficiencies: lack of independence, absence of executive sessions, and failure to remediate identified control weaknesses. A candidate selecting this option is treating a symptom (overburdened committee) that is not supported by the facts — the committee meets the minimum standard — rather than identifying the substantive governance failures described in the scenario.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Adding two more independent members to expand the committee to five would increase committee size without resolving the root causes of governance failure. Stock exchange listing standards require a minimum of three members, which SignalPoint already satisfies. The deficiencies identified are qualitative: two of three members lack independence (the CEO's brother and a recently retired operations manager), the committee has never held an executive session, and significant deficiencies have gone un-remediated for two years. Adding more members does not change the existing members' compromised independence, does not automatically create a culture of executive sessions, and does not compel action on the revenue recognition deficiency. A candidate selecting this option confuses committee size with committee effectiveness — adding independent members to a dysfunctional committee without removing the non-independent members or changing processes is an incomplete and ineffective recommendation.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Removing the CEO from audit committee meetings addresses only one symptom of a multi-faceted governance failure. While management's presence can inhibit candid discussion between the committee and external auditors, correcting this single issue leaves unresolved: the CEO's brother's continued presence on the committee (a direct SOX Section 301 independence violation), the retired operations manager's potential lack of objectivity after only two years' separation, and the committee's two-year failure to insist on remediation of the revenue recognition significant deficiency. Executive sessions without management present are a necessary safeguard, but they cannot compensate for a committee whose composition is fundamentally compromised by a disqualifying family relationship. A candidate selecting this option is oversimplifying the governance failure — treating a single procedural deficiency as the sole cause when the scenario describes systematic failures in composition, process, and accountability.",
