    "ExplanationWrongA": "Isolation Forest detected all 14 confirmed issues — a complete true-positive set. However, the 88.7% false-positive rate makes this method operationally unusable for monthly close. Investigating 124 flagged transactions to find 14 genuine issues requires processing 7.9 false positives per true positive. At an estimated 15 minutes per investigation, the 110 false positives consume 27.5 hours of FP&A analyst time — approximately 3.5 business days — to identify only $4,300 in additional errors beyond what IQR caught. The governance principle is that detection methods must be operationally sustainable: an 88.7% false-positive rate cannot be sustained month after month because investigation fatigue will lead analysts to reduce scrutiny on all flags, potentially missing genuine errors in future months. For management accountants evaluating detection methods, false-positive rate is as important as true-positive rate.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Z-Score produced the smallest investigation burden (47 flags) and the 31 legitimate quarterly royalty payments can indeed be whitelisted. However, Z-Score found ZERO confirmed errors — not a single duplicate payment, incorrect conversion, or other anomaly requiring correction. If Z-Score had been used as the sole detection method, the $47,200 in duplicate payments (caught by IQR) would have gone undetected. The reason Z-Score missed the duplicates is mathematical: duplicate payments have amounts identical to legitimate payments (they are duplicates of real invoices), meaning their Z-Score is near zero — they do not deviate from the mean at all. Z-Score is designed to detect unusually large or small payments, not duplicate payments. This illustrates why multiple detection methods should be evaluated against the specific anomaly type being targeted: outlier detection for amount anomalies versus pattern detection for duplicates.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "The combined IQR + DBSCAN approach has analytical merit — DBSCAN's 2 additional duplicate payment detections ($4,300) represent errors that no other method caught. However, DBSCAN's incremental contribution (2 detections, $4,300) must be weighed against the cost of running and investigating a second method. DBSCAN flagged 22 anomalous payments across 4 clusters — 18 of which were the same duplicates IQR already caught. This means 18 of 22 DBSCAN investigations (82%) were redundant with IQR. For the $4,300 in additional detection, Summit would need to maintain and run DBSCAN monthly — the operational cost of running a clustering algorithm on 142,000 transactions likely exceeds the incremental detection value. The FP&A principle is that detection methods should be additive, not redundant: a supplementary method is justified only when it detects a material number of errors that the primary method misses, not when it re-detects the primary method's findings.",
